Design of the experiment (bounded, deterministic, cheap):

Goal: Test whether the proposed clause-local R12 fix (patched tool 5eaab3f8) generalizes beyond the exact token-triggered phrasing, i.e., measure the lexical ceiling:
- Generate a set of leak paraphrases that assert C2-type inextendibility (or otherwise class-crossing content) *without* the tokens the R12 exemption keys on (inextendib, extension, horizon, ...).
- For each: run canonical base (000e09e4), patched proposal (5eaab3f8), and independent flash11 gate (a89b221c) on a byte-minimal fixture derived from frozen rev29 WCC (d9cebb9404b2) — each fixture = base + one appended sentence in non_vacuity.condition.
- Controls: legitimate geodesic statements (must remain accepted by patched) + the frozen canonical schema itself.
- Report per-probe accept/reject + rule ids for each gate, plus a classification: CAUGHT (leak rejected) / ESCAPED (leak accepted).
- Falsifier: if any non-lexical rephrasing that a human would call a class crossing is accepted by the *patched* gate → lexical ceiling confirmed; the patch is a token patch, not a semantic fix. If all rephrasings are caught by the patched gate → the lexical-ceiling hypothesis is falsified (at this probe set), which strengthens the patch proposal.
- Also measure base gate: expect it accepts all (exemption fires) → shows the leak class is systematic.

This is a good artifact-backed bounded task: ~15-25 probe fixtures × 3 gates; fast.

